now this week china imposed sanctions on  10  uk organizations and individuals for  spreading what it calls  lies and disinformation about human  rights abuses now the sanctions hit  five conservative mps including the  former minister  mr ghani who joins us now and what  happened  well it's a curious state of affairs  sophie a number of us were  sanctioned and i think that chinese  communist parties thought that they were  intimidating us or were going to stop us  talking about gross human rights abuses  particularly  in xinjiang but it's been a complete  misstep by the chinese communist party  it's done a couple of things instead  it's actually made parliament come  together  and say we won't have democratically  elected officials intimidated anyway  it's also sophie given us an opportunity  to talk about the one thing  the chinese communist party do not want  us to talk about which is the gross  human rights abuses against the uyghur  people and the fact that a genocide has  been called out  against china on the way that they're  treating the weaker people by many  countries around the world  you of course spoken out very strongly  about some of those human rights  abuses i just wonder if you feel that  the uk government has been tough enough  in its dealings with china  if you look at for example the  integrated defense review published a  couple of weeks ago  it calls for a positive trade and  investment relationship  with china given the treatment of the  uyghurs given the situation in hong kong  given the fact this is a country that  has sanctioned british mps  is that really good enough well we must  remember these sanctions are in  retaliation for the uk government  sanctioning chinese officials who have  been  implicated in the gross human rights  abuses so the uk government is  definitely in the right direction we  would ask  many of us colleagues will ask that the  uk government goes even further  and sanctions even more chinese  officials who are involved in the abuses  in  xinjiang and what's also come about  because of the sanctions sophie  a greater discussion about where we  stand  in the new global order if the chinese  communist party is going to be using  both its  financial power through its belt and  road initiative and also  using these so-called sanctions to try  and silence individuals like myself  i think these sanctions are a turning  point in how we consider our long-term  relationship  with china but not just us individually  just the uk but also working with our  partners countries like america for  example  i think this is a moment that i don't  expect the chinese communist party  wanted to come about because of the  sanctions  but we now have an opportunity to think  about are we going to assert our values  and how we're going to do that  now boris johnson hosted the mps had  been sanctioned  yesterday in downing street couldn't  help but notice that tom tuggenheim  wasn't there  the chair of the foreign affairs select  committee who also was sanctioned  um he tweeted to say he wasn't invited  do you know what happened the meeting  was organized  um very quickly um at the last minute  because the sanctions came about over  overnight on thursday we were made aware  of them on friday and the meeting came  about very quickly  on on saturday but look all of us  colleagues  were sanctioned for doing what we've  been elected to do  which so was he invited do you know we  were all invited at very  very um last minute sophie but what's  what's critical here is that we met with  the prime minister and the prime  minister's standing  side by side with us which i don't think  was the outcome the chinese communist  party thought they were going to achieve  with the sanctions i assume they thought  that we would be  marginalized by our party and that we  will then feel intimidated in talking  about the abuse of the uyghur  but that definitely is not what has  happened just feels bit strange  you know that he tweeted to say he  wasn't invited he wasn't there he's  obviously one of the most high profile  critics of china and the uyghurs you  know it'd be pretty bad look obviously  for the prime minister to be speaking  free speech but not inviting tom tugan  perhaps because of things that he said  so for the best of your knowledge that's  not the case he was invited  people were invited at very last minutes  i mean another parliamentary colleague  was in leicestershire  wasn't able to get down in time um but  the story here sophie is that we are  not going to be intimidated we're  continuing to speak about the abuse of  the weaker and we now have an even  closer relationship with the officials  in our government whether it's with the  prime minister or whether it's the  foreign secretary who are going to be  even more vocal because of the meeting  that took place yesterday  and the statements the foreign secretary  made so whatever outcome the chinese  communist party thought they were going  to achieve with sanctioning  elected officials that definitely hasn't  happened and all we've been able to do  is talk about the abuse  of the uyghur and if i have a moment  sophie is talking about two million  people that are in prison camps  in xinjiang and it is an issue for us  because they are forced into labor  and those products eventually earned up  on our shelves here in the uk  yeah a really important point to get  across and it's pretty clear from  speaking to you that the sanctions are  not going to  silence you if that is the aim and just  very finely while i have you  and we're talking about the road map out  of lockdown  the government asked parliament to  extend the emergency code restrictions  for another  six months earlier this week and you  abstained in that vote  um why did you abstain well we know that  the road map concludes towards the end  of  june and you know the government's doing  a fantastic job on that but there was a  particular  issue within the legislation which is  that the powers were to extend  up to october which is three months  beyond the road map and  i just felt that that was unnecessary  there was every opportunity  for parliamentarians to come back to the  house and for the executives to put  forward their position  and for that debate to take place we  know that the roadmap will will conclude  in a couple of months time of course the  government needs flexibility  but i just thought it was unnecessary  for for those rules and regulations  those powers that exist elsewhere in  other bits of legislation  to be in position for three months  beyond um the roadmap but look  um you know in in sussex we've had one  million people vaccinated so i'm just  really pleased that the vaccination  program is going incredibly well  and we're getting towards the end of um  this covered  lockdown and we'll be out of it by the  end of june yeah you can meet up with  five other people outside from tomorrow  it will be incredible very exciting news  and nisra garnier thank you very much  for being on the program today thank you  thank you
 

